{"step_index":11,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:06:47Z","content":"Absolutely Here are Thoracic Surgeries in the same order using simple exam oriented language THORACIC SURGERIES --- **1 Thoracoplasty** **Definition** Thoracoplasty is a surgical procedure in which part of the ribs is removed to collapse the chest wall over a diseased part of the lung helping the lung cavity to close **Etiology / Indications** • Old pulmonary tuberculosis with persistent cavity • Chronic empyema • Bronchopleural fistula • Persistent infected pleural cavity • Destroyed lung when other procedures are unsuitable **Contraindications** • Severe respiratory insufficiency • Extensive bilateral lung disease • Patient unfit for major surgery • Active uncontrolled infection **Risk Factors** • Chronic pulmonary TB • Poor nutrition • Chronic infection • Smoking • Previous thoracic surgery **Types** 1 Complete thoracoplasty extensive rib removal 2 Partial thoracoplasty only selected ribs are removed 3 Apical thoracoplasty mainly upper ribs are removed to collapse the apical cavity **Surgical Procedure** • Incision is made over the chest wall • Selected ribs are exposed • Portions of ribs are removed • Chest wall collapses inward • The underlying cavity is compressed **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pulmonary function tests • Sputum examination • Blood investigations **Management** • Anti tubercular treatment when indicated • Antibiotics for infection • Pain management • Respiratory physiotherapy • Breathing exercises • Gradual mobilization **Complications** • Chest deformity • Reduced chest expansion • Restrictive respiratory impairment • Pain • Infection • Bleeding • Poor shoulder movement --- **2 Pulmonary Resection Surgery** **Definition** Pulmonary resection is surgery in which a diseased portion of the lung is removed **Etiology / Indications** • Lung cancer • Bronchiectasis • Lung abscess • Localized tuberculosis • Pulmonary cysts • Localized destroyed lung **Contraindications** • Very poor lung function • Extensive bilateral disease • Severe cardiac disease • Patient unable to tolerate anesthesia/surgery **Risk Factors** • Smoking • Chronic lung disease • TB • Recurrent infections • Poor nutritional status **Types** • Wedge resection small wedge shaped portion removed • Segmentectomy one anatomical segment removed • Lobectomy one lobe removed • Pneumonectomy entire lung removed **Surgical Procedure** • Chest is opened or minimally invasive access is used • Diseased lung portion is identified • Blood vessels and bronchus are divided and secured • Diseased tissue is removed • Chest drain is usually placed **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pulmonary function tests • Bronchoscopy • ABG • ECG • Blood tests **Management** • Oxygen when required • Chest physiotherapy • Deep breathing exercises • Supported coughing • Early mobilization • Pain control • Chest drain management **Complications** • Air leak • Bleeding • Infection • Atelectasis • Pneumonia • Respiratory failure --- **3 Thoracotomy** **Definition** Thoracotomy is a surgical incision made through the chest wall to gain access to the lungs pleura heart or other thoracic structures Important Thoracotomy is an approach/incision not necessarily removal of lung tissue **Indications** • Lung surgery • Esophageal surgery • Pleural surgery • Chest trauma • Tumor removal • Decortication **Contraindications** • Patient unable to tolerate major surgery • Severe uncontrolled medical illness • Severe respiratory insufficiency **Risk Factors** Depend on the underlying disease • Smoking • COPD • Poor lung function • Infection • Malignancy **Types** 1 Posterolateral thoracotomy 2 Anterolateral thoracotomy 3 Axillary thoracotomy 4 Median sternotomy commonly used for cardiac surgery although technically different from thoracotomy **Surgical Procedure** • Patient is positioned usually on the side • Incision is made over the chest • Muscles are divided or separated • Ribs are spread • Thoracic cavity is entered • Required surgery is performed • Chest is closed with a drain when needed **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • PFT • ECG • ABG • Blood tests **Management** • Pain control • Breathing exercises • Supported coughing • Chest physiotherapy • Early mobilization • Shoulder ROM exercises **Complications** • Severe pain • Atelectasis • Pneumonia • Wound infection • Air leak • Respiratory complications • Shoulder stiffness --- **4 Lobectomy** **Definition** Lobectomy is the surgical removal of one lobe of the lung **Indications** • Localized lung cancer • Bronchiectasis • Lung abscess • Tuberculosis • Congenital lung disease **Contraindications** • Inadequate remaining lung function • Severe cardiac/respiratory disease • Extensive disease affecting remaining lung **Risk Factors** • Smoking • COPD • Poor nutritional status • Previous thoracic disease • Older age **Types** • Right upper lobectomy • Right middle lobectomy • Right lower lobectomy • Left upper lobectomy • Left lower lobectomy **Surgical Procedure** • Chest is accessed • Pulmonary vessels supplying the lobe are divided • Lobar bronchus is divided • Diseased lobe is removed • Chest drain is inserted • Incision is closed **Investigations** • CT chest • Chest X ray • PFT • Bronchoscopy • ECG • ABG **Management** • Pain relief • Breathing exercises • Incentive spirometry • Supported coughing • Chest physiotherapy • Early mobilization **Complications** • Air leak • Atelectasis • Pneumonia • Bleeding • Infection • Respiratory failure --- **5 Pneumonectomy** **Definition** Pneumonectomy is the surgical removal of an entire lung **Indications** • Central lung cancer • Extensive bronchiectasis • Destroyed lung • Severe localized TB • Extensive unilateral lung disease **Contraindications** • Poor remaining lung function • Severe pulmonary hypertension • Severe cardiac disease • Bilateral extensive lung disease **Risk Factors** • Smoking • COPD • Poor cardiovascular function • Older age • Poor nutritional status **Types** 1 Simple pneumonectomy removal of one lung 2 Extrapleural pneumonectomy more extensive removal including pleural structures used in selected conditions such as some mesotheliomas **Surgical Procedure** • Thoracic cavity is opened • Pulmonary artery and veins are divided • Main bronchus is divided and closed • Entire lung is removed • Chest is closed with appropriate drainage/management **Investigations** • PFT • CT chest • Chest X ray • ECG • Echocardiography • ABG • Bronchoscopy **Management** • Oxygen as required • Breathing exercises • Chest physiotherapy • Early mobilization • Pain control • Monitoring of fluid balance **Complications** • Respiratory failure • Arrhythmias • Infection • Bleeding • Post pneumonectomy syndrome • Cardiac complications --- **6 Decortication** **Definition** Decortication is a surgical procedure in which a thick fibrous layer covering the lung is removed so that the lung can expand again **Indications** • Chronic empyema • Organized hemothorax • Fibrothorax • Chronic pleural infection • Trapped lung **Contraindications** • Patient unfit for surgery • Severe irreversible lung disease • Uncontrolled systemic illness **Risk Factors** • Chronic empyema • Tuberculosis • Pneumonia • Hemothorax • Previous pleural infection **Types** • Open decortication • VATS decortication video assisted thoracoscopic surgery **Surgical Procedure** • Chest is entered • Thick fibrous pleural layer is identified • Fibrous peel is carefully removed • Lung is allowed to re expand • Chest drains are inserted **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pleural fluid analysis • Blood tests • Pulmonary function tests **Management** • Antibiotics • Chest drainage • Breathing exercises • Incentive spirometry • Supported coughing • Early mobilization **Complications** • Bleeding • Air leak • Infection • Persistent trapped lung • Respiratory failure • Pain --- **7 Surgery for Pneumothorax** **Definition** Pneumothorax is the presence of air in the pleural space causing partial or complete collapse of the lung **Etiology** • Spontaneous rupture of lung bleb • Trauma • Mechanical ventilation • Lung disease • Iatrogenic causes **Indications for Surgery** • Recurrent pneumothorax • Persistent air leak • Bilateral pneumothorax in selected cases • First episode in selected high risk situations • Failure of conservative treatment **Contraindications** • Surgery may be avoided when pneumothorax is small and stable and resolves with conservative management **Risk Factors** • Smoking • COPD • Previous pneumothorax • Mechanical ventilation • Lung disease **Types** • Primary spontaneous • Secondary spontaneous • Traumatic • Iatrogenic • Tension pneumothorax **Surgical Procedure** Common procedures include • VATS • Removal/stapling of leaking blebs or bullae • Pleurodesis to prevent recurrence • Sometimes pleurectomy **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pulse oximetry • ABG when required **Management** • Observation for selected small stable cases • Oxygen when appropriate • Needle decompression for tension pneumothorax • Intercostal chest tube • Surgery for recurrent/persistent cases **Complications** • Recurrence • Persistent air leak • Infection • Respiratory failure • Tension pneumothorax --- **8 Surgery for Hydropneumothorax** **Definition** Hydropneumothorax is the presence of both air and fluid in the pleural cavity **Etiology** • Trauma • TB • Infection • Ruptured lung abscess • Esophageal rupture • Complication of procedures **Indications for Surgery** • Large collection • Persistent air/fluid leak • Infection • Trapped lung • Failure of chest tube drainage **Contraindications** • Small stable collection responding to conservative treatment • Patient unfit for surgery **Risk Factors** • TB • Chest trauma • Pleural infection • Previous thoracic procedure **Types** • Simple hydropneumothorax • Infected hydropneumothorax **Surgical Procedure** • Intercostal chest tube is inserted to drain fluid and air • VATS may be performed when drainage is inadequate • Decortication or repair of the underlying cause may be required **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pleural fluid analysis • Blood tests • Culture **Management** • Chest drainage • Antibiotics if infection is present • Treatment of underlying disease • Surgery if persistent/complicated **Complications** • Empyema • Fibrothorax • Persistent air leak • Sepsis • Respiratory failure --- **9 Surgery for Empyema** **Definition** Empyema is the collection of pus/infected fluid in the pleural cavity **Etiology** • Bacterial pneumonia • TB • Lung abscess • Chest surgery • Chest trauma **Indications for Surgery** • Loculated empyema • Thick pleural peel • Failure of antibiotics and drainage • Trapped lung • Chronic empyema **Contraindications** • Patient unable to tolerate surgery • Very small uncomplicated collection responding to treatment **Risk Factors** • Pneumonia • TB • Diabetes • Immunosuppression • Chest trauma • Previous surgery **Types** 1 Acute empyema 2 Chronic/organized empyema **Surgical Procedure** Depending on stage • Chest tube drainage • VATS drainage • Break loculations • Decortication for organized empyema • Open drainage in selected chronic cases **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pleural fluid analysis • Gram stain and culture • CBC • CRP/ESR **Management** • Antibiotics • Chest drainage • Nutritional support • Respiratory physiotherapy • VATS or decortication when required **Complications** • Sepsis • Fibrothorax • Trapped lung • Bronchopleural fistula • Respiratory failure --- **10 Pulmonary Tuberculosis Surgery** **Definition** Pulmonary TB surgery refers to surgical removal or drainage of localized damaged or complicated lung tissue due to tuberculosis usually when medical treatment alone is insufficient **Indications** • Localized destroyed lung • Persistent cavity • Massive hemoptysis from localized disease • Bronchiectasis due to TB • Drug resistant localized disease in selected cases • Persistent complications despite adequate treatment **Contraindications** • Extensive bilateral disease • Poor respiratory reserve • Unfit patient • Active uncontrolled systemic disease **Risk Factors** • Poor nutrition • Smoking • Immunosuppression • Delayed diagnosis • Drug resistant TB **Types** • Segmentectomy • Lobectomy • Pneumonectomy • Thoracoplasty • Cavity procedures in selected cases **Surgical Procedure** Depends on disease • Diseased segment/lobe may be removed • In severely destroyed unilateral lung pneumonectomy may be considered • Thoracoplasty may be used in selected chronic cavities **Investigations** • Sputum examination • GeneXpert/NAAT • Chest X ray • CT chest • Bronchoscopy • PFT • Blood tests **Management** • Anti tubercular therapy is the main treatment • Surgery is used only in selected complicated cases • Nutritional support • Respiratory physiotherapy • Exercise rehabilitation **Complications** • Hemoptysis • Bronchopleural fistula • Empyema • Respiratory failure • Postoperative infection • Recurrence --- **11 Lung Transplant** **Definition** Lung transplantation is a surgery in which a diseased lung or lungs are replaced by healthy donor lung tissue **Indications** • End stage COPD • Cystic fibrosis • Interstitial lung disease • Pulmonary hypertension • Other severe irreversible lung diseases **Contraindications** • Active untreated infection • Active malignancy • Severe irreversible dysfunction of another major organ • Inability to tolerate transplantation or long term immunosuppression **Risk Factors for Poor Outcome** • Severe infection • Poor nutritional status • Renal dysfunction • Cardiovascular disease • Poor adherence to treatment **Types** 1 Single lung transplant 2 Double/bilateral lung transplant 3 Combined heart lung transplant **Surgical Procedure** • Diseased lung is removed • Donor lung is placed • Pulmonary artery and pulmonary veins are connected • Bronchus is connected • Blood flow and ventilation are checked • Chest is closed **Investigations** • PFT • CT chest • Blood tests • ECG • Echocardiography • Infection screening • Tissue compatibility assessment **Management** • Lifelong immunosuppressive medication • Infection prevention • Regular lung function monitoring • Pulmonary rehabilitation • Breathing exercises • Exercise training **Complications** • Rejection • Infection • Bleeding • Airway complications • Primary graft dysfunction • Chronic lung allograft dysfunction --- **12 Pleurectomy** **Definition** Pleurectomy is the surgical removal of part or all of the pleura the membrane surrounding the lungs **Indications** • Recurrent pneumothorax • Pleural tumors • Some cases of malignant pleural disease • Chronic pleural infection in selected cases **Contraindications** • Severe respiratory/cardiac disease • Patient unfit for surgery • Extensive disease where surgery is not beneficial **Risk Factors** • Recurrent pneumothorax • Smoking • Pleural malignancy • Chronic pleural infection • Previous thoracic disease **Types** 1 Partial pleurectomy part of pleura is removed 2 Total pleurectomy extensive removal of pleura 3 Pleurectomy/decortication pleura and fibrous peel are removed in selected diseases **Surgical Procedure** • Chest is accessed often using VATS or open thoracotomy • Diseased pleura is identified • Pleura is carefully separated and removed • Chest drains are placed • Chest is closed **Investigations** • Chest X ray • CT chest • Pleural fluid analysis • Pleural biopsy • PFT • Blood tests **Management** • Pain control • Chest drainage • Breathing exercises • Incentive spirometry • Supported coughing • Early mobilization • Treatment of underlying disease **Complications** • Bleeding • Air leak • Infection • Persistent pain • Respiratory complications • Reduced lung expansion --- **Quick Revision** 1 Thoracoplasty removal of ribs to collapse chest wall/cavity 2 Pulmonary resection removal of diseas