{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:28:34Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. ARTHRODESIS Definition Arthrodesis is a surgical procedure in which a joint is deliberately fused by removing articular cartilage and stabilizing the joint until bony union occurs, permanently eliminating movement at that joint. Objectives • Relieve severe pain • Provide a stable joint • Correct deformity • Improve function • Prevent further joint destruction Indications • Severe osteoarthritis or rheumatoid arthritis • Post-traumatic arthritis • Joint destruction after trauma • Selected controlled infective or tuberculous arthritis • Severe joint instability • Paralytic instability • Congenital deformity • Failed reconstruction or joint replacement • When joint replacement is unsuitable Contraindications • Active uncontrolled infection • Poor vascularity • Severe osteoporosis • Poor general health preventing surgery • Inadequate soft-tissue coverage • Severe disease of adjacent joints Classification • By site: spinal, shoulder, elbow, wrist, hip, knee, ankle and small-joint arthrodesis • By technique: extra-articular, intra-articular and combined • By fixation: cast/immobilization, internal fixation, external fixation or combined Principles • Adequate exposure • Remove diseased cartilage • Prepare subchondral bone • Correct deformity • Position joint in a functional position • Obtain good bone-to-bone contact • Provide stable fixation• Use bone graft when required • Preserve blood supply • Immobilize until solid union General surgical procedure • Expose joint • Remove cartilage and diseased tissue • Freshen bone surfaces • Correct deformity • Position surfaces in functional alignment • Insert bone graft when indicated • Fix with screws, plates, nails or other fixation • Close wound and immobilize Postoperative & physiotherapy management • Pain and edema control • Wound care and radiographic monitoring • Respiratory/circulatory exercises when indicated • Active movement of uninvolved joints • Isometric exercises where permitted • Progressive strengthening • Transfer and gait training • Progressive weight bearing only when permitted • Functional and ADL training Complications • Infection • Delayed union • Non-union • Malunion • Implant failure • Neurovascular injury • Adjacent-joint degeneration • Persistent pain • Limb-length discrepancy in selected procedures • Reduced overall mobility Conclusion Arthrodesis is a useful salvage procedure for a severely painful, unstable or destroyed joint. Proper positioning, adequate bone preparation, stable fixation and solid bony union are essential.2. ARTHROPLASTY — PARTIAL AND TOTAL JOINT REPLACEMENT Definition Arthroplasty is a surgical procedure to reconstruct, reshape or replace a diseased or damaged joint to relieve pain and restore movement and function. Joint replacement arthroplasty uses artificial components to replace damaged joint surfaces. Objectives • Pain relief • Restore joint movement • Correct deformity • Improve stability • Restore function • Improve quality of life Classification • Partial arthroplasty: one component/articular surface is replaced, e.g. hip hemiarthroplasty or unicompartmental knee replacement • Total arthroplasty: both major articulating surfaces are replaced, e.g. total hip or total knee replacement • By fixation: cemented, uncemented or hybrid • By material: metal, polyethylene, ceramic or combinations Indications • Severe osteoarthritis • Rheumatoid arthritis • Avascular necrosis • Severe joint destruction • Selected fractures • Failed previous surgery • Severe pain unresponsive to conservative treatment • Severe deformity • Major loss of function Contraindications • Active joint infection • Severe uncontrolled infection • Inadequate bone stock • Severe neurological dysfunction affecting stability • Poor soft-tissue condition • Severe medical illness preventing surgery • Inability to follow postoperative precautions Principles • Correct patient selection• Adequate exposure • Remove diseased surfaces • Preserve bone stock • Accurate bone preparation • Correct component positioning • Restore alignment and soft-tissue balance • Secure fixation • Prevent infection • Early controlled mobilization • Long-term prosthesis protection Partial replacement • Only the damaged component is replaced • Example: hip hemiarthroplasty replaces the femoral head/neck while retaining the acetabulum • Advantages: less bone removal and useful in selected fractures • Disadvantage: the remaining natural surface may later degenerate Total replacement • Both major articulating surfaces are replaced • Total hip: femoral and acetabular components • Total knee: femoral and tibial components; patellar resurfacing may be performed depending on the procedure Physiotherapy management • Positioning and pain/edema control • Breathing exercises and ankle pumps • Quadriceps and gluteal setting • ROM as permitted • Bed mobility and transfer training • Early standing and walking with prescribed weight bearing • Progressive strengthening • Gait, stairs, balance and ADL training • Patient education and home programme Complications • Infection • DVT • Pulmonary embolism • Bleeding • Neurovascular injury • Dislocation in susceptible hip replacements • Prosthetic loosening • Wear • Periprosthetic fracture• Instability • Reduced ROM • Revision surgery Conclusion Arthroplasty aims to produce a pain-free, stable and functional joint. Rehabilitation requires respect for precautions, early safe mobilization, progressive strengthening and functional retraining.3. OSTEOTOMY Definition Osteotomy is a surgical procedure in which a bone is deliberately cut and then realigned, reshaped or repositioned to correct deformity, redistribute load or improve function. Objectives • Correct angular deformity • Correct rotational deformity • Address selected limb-length discrepancies • Redistribute abnormal joint loading • Improve alignment • Reduce pain • Improve function • Delay or avoid joint replacement in selected patients Indications • Congenital/developmental deformity • Post-traumatic malalignment • Varus or valgus deformity • Rotational deformity • Abnormal mechanical axis • Selected osteoarthritis • Limb-length discrepancy • Malunion • Joint-preserving surgery in suitable patients Classification • Corrective osteotomy • Derotational osteotomy • Lengthening osteotomy • Shortening osteotomy • Realignment osteotomy • Techniques: closing-wedge, opening-wedge, dome and step-cut osteotomy Principles • Accurate assessment of deformity • Identify anatomical and mechanical axes • Calculate required correction • Select appropriate site • Preserve blood supply and soft tissues • Perform controlled bone cuts • Correct alignment accurately • Maintain suitable bone contact• Use appropriate fixation • Protect neurovascular structures • Rehabilitate according to healing General procedure • Preoperative radiographic planning • Calculate correction • Anaesthesia and exposure • Perform osteotomy • Reposition bone segments • Check alignment • Bone graft when indicated • Fix with plate/screws, intramedullary device or external fixator • Close wound and protect limb Physiotherapy management • Pain and edema control • Positioning • Circulatory and respiratory exercises when indicated • Maintain permitted ROM • Isometric strengthening • Transfer and assistive-device training • Protected gait • After healing: progressive weight bearing, resistance, gait, balance, proprioception and functional training Complications • Infection • Delayed union • Non-union • Malunion • Neurovascular injury • Compartment syndrome • Loss of correction • Implant failure • Joint stiffness • Thromboembolic complications • Persistent pain Conclusion Osteotomy is an important deformity-correcting and joint-preserving procedure. Accurate planning, controlled bone cutting and maintenance of alignment are essential.4. EXTERNAL FIXATORS Definition An external fixator is an orthopaedic stabilization device in which pins, wires or screws are inserted into bone and connected outside the body by a rigid frame. Indications • Open fractures • Severe soft-tissue injury • Polytrauma • Unstable or comminuted fractures • Severe swelling • Bone loss • Infected non-union • Limb lengthening • Bone transport • Deformity correction • Temporary stabilization • Damage-control orthopaedics • Selected paediatric fractures • Selected arthrodesis Classification • Unilateral/monolateral fixator • Bilateral fixator • Circular/ring fixator • Hybrid fixator • Common systems include unilateral, Ilizarov-type circular and hybrid frames Components • Schanz pins/half-pins • K-wires in some systems • Connecting rods • Clamps • Rings • Struts • Hinges where required Principles • Stabilize bone fragments • Maintain alignment and limb length • Preserve fracture biology and soft tissues • Avoid neurovascular injury • Use safe pin corridors• Provide adequate frame stability • Allow wound access when necessary • Maintain pin-site hygiene • Progress rehabilitation according to stability and healing Advantages • Rapid application • Useful in severe soft-tissue injury • Useful for open and complex fractures • Allows wound access • Can be adjusted • Useful for lengthening and deformity correction Disadvantages • Pin-site infection • Bulky frame • Patient discomfort • Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Difficulty with ADLs • Pin loosening • Possible neurovascular injury Physiotherapy management • Breathing/circulatory exercises • ROM of free joints • Quadriceps/gluteal isometrics as appropriate • Bed mobility and transfers • Gait training with prescribed assistive device • Progressive strengthening and ROM • Weight bearing only as prescribed • Balance training • Pin-site care education Complications • Pin-site infection • Pin loosening • Osteomyelitis • Joint stiffness • Frame instability • Loss of alignment • Delayed union • Non-union• Malunion • Device failure • Nerve/vessel injury • Compartment syndrome Conclusion External fixation is particularly valuable when fracture stabilization and soft-tissue management must be addressed together. Physiotherapy maintains mobility, strength and function while protecting the frame.5. SPINAL STABILIZATION SURGERIES Definition Spinal stabilization surgery is performed to restore or maintain spinal alignment and stability by fixing one or more vertebral segments, often with instrumentation and bone grafting. Indications • Spinal instability • Progressive scoliosis • Severe kyphosis • Vertebral fractures • Spinal deformity • Spondylolisthesis • Tumours • Severe degenerative instability • Selected spinal infections • Neurological compromise associated with instability • Failed conservative management Objectives • Correct deformity • Maintain alignment • Stabilize unstable vertebrae • Prevent progression • Facilitate fusion • Reduce mechanical pain • Protect neurological structures Harrington's rod system • Older spinal instrumentation using rods to correct and maintain spinal deformity, particularly scoliosis • Principle: corrective forces are applied to the spine and maintained while fusion occurs • General procedure: posterior exposure, instrumentation/hooks, rod placement, deformity correction, bone grafting and closure • Historically important but largely replaced by newer segmental fixation systems Luque's rod system • Uses contoured rods with sublaminar wires for segmental spinal fixation • Historically used for scoliosis, neuromuscular deformity and selected kyphotic/unstable conditions • Provides segmental fixation and control of alignment • Complications include neurological injury, infection, implant failure, loss of correction and pseudarthrosis Steffee plating • Older segmental spinal fixation method using plates and screws, especially for lumbar stabilization • Pedicle screws are connected by longitudinal plates to stabilize the spine and facilitate fusion • Used in selected lumbar instability, spondylolisthesis, deformity, fractures and pseudarthrosis• Complications include screw malposition, neurological/vascular injury, infection, implant failure and non-union General principles • Proper patient selection • Identify unstable segments • Correct deformity where necessary • Decompress when indicated • Provide stable fixation • Use bone graft to facilitate fusion • Preserve neurological and vascular structures • Maintain sagittal and coronal alignment • Protect spine postoperatively • Begin safe mobilization and progressive rehabilitation Physiotherapy management • Preoperative respiratory, neurological, postural and strength assessment • Patient education and postoperative mobility training • Early postoperative breathing/circulatory exercises • Log rolling and safe transfers • Appropriate ambulation • Later: progressive walking, lower-limb strengthening, balance and functional training • Core stabilization only when permitted • Avoid excessive flexion, twisting, heavy lifting and high-impact activity during healing unless cleared Complications • Infection • Neurological deficit • Dural injury • Implant failure • Pseudarthrosis • Loss of correction • Persistent pain • Adjacent-segment disease • Spinal stiffness • Thromboembolic complications Conclusion Spinal stabilization aims to restore stability, correct deformity and achieve solid fusion. Harrington and Luque systems are historically important; modern fixation generally uses more advanced segmental instrumentation.6. LIMB RE-ATTACHMENT / LIMB REPLANTATION Definition Limb replantation is a microsurgical procedure in which a completely amputated limb or part is surgically reattached with restoration of bone, vascular, nerve, tendon and soft-tissue continuity. Objectives • Restore limb continuity • Restore blood circulation • Preserve maximum limb length • Restore sensation • Restore motor function • Achieve skeletal union • Obtain useful functional recovery Indications • Major limb amputation in selected patients • Thumb amputation • Multiple-digit amputation • Amputation through the palm • Paediatric amputation • Multiple-digit injury • Selected proximal upper-limb amputations • Clean-cut amputation with a viable amputated part Poor candidates / relative contraindications • Severe crush injury • Severe contamination • Prolonged ischaemia • Extensive tissue destruction • Life-threatening associated injuries • Unsuitable amputated part • Severe medical instability Classification • Complete vs incomplete amputation • By level: finger, hand, wrist, forearm, elbow, arm; toe, foot, ankle, leg, knee, thigh • By mechanism: clean-cut, crush, avulsion and combined crush-avulsion Principles • Resuscitate and control bleeding • Preserve amputated part correctly • Debride nonviable tissue • Prepare/shorten bone when required • Restore skeletal stability• Repair tendons • Repair arteries and veins • Repair nerves • Provide soft-tissue coverage • Monitor circulation closely Preservation of amputated part • Wrap in sterile moist gauze • Place in a clean waterproof bag • Place the bag in an ice/cold-water mixture • Do not place the amputated part directly on ice or directly in water Surgical sequence • Debridement • Bone preparation and fixation • Tendon repair • Arterial microsurgical anastomosis • Venous repair • Nerve repair • Skin/soft-tissue coverage • Postoperative vascular monitoring Physiotherapy management • Protect repairs and control edema • Maintain safe-joint mobility • Respiratory/circulatory exercises • Appropriate isometrics • Progressive ROM after healing • Tendon-gliding where appropriate • Strengthening • Sensory re-education • Scar and edema management • Fine-motor training for upper limb • Gait training for lower limb • Proprioception and functional/return-to-work training Complications • Vascular thrombosis • Arterial occlusion • Venous congestion • Infection • Compartment syndrome • Tissue necrosis• Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Tendon adhesions • Non-union/malunion • Cold intolerance • Neuroma • Poor sensory/motor recovery • Chronic pain • Functional limitation Conclusion Limb replantation is a complex multidisciplinary microsurgical procedure. Outcome depends on injury level and mechanism, ischaemia time, tissue viability, quality of repair and prolonged rehabilitation.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T15:58:34+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
