aintenance of alignment are essential --- **4 EXTERNAL FIXATORS** **Definition** An external fixator is an orthopaedic stabilization device in which pins wires or screws are inserted into bone and connected outside the body by a rigid frame **Indications** • Open fractures • Severe soft tissue injury • Polytrauma • Unstable or comminuted fractures • Severe swelling • Bone loss • Infected non union • Limb lengthening • Bone transport • Deformity correction • Temporary stabilization • Damage control orthopaedics • Selected paediatric fractures • Selected arthrodesis **Classification** • Unilateral/monolateral fixator • Bilateral fixator • Circular/ring fixator • Hybrid fixator • Common systems include unilateral Ilizarov type circular and hybrid frames **Components** • Schanz pins/half pins • K wires in some systems • Connecting rods • Clamps • Rings • Struts • Hinges where required **Principles** • Stabilize bone fragments • Maintain alignment and limb length • Preserve fracture biology and soft tissues • Avoid neurovascular injury • Use safe pin corridors • Provide adequate frame stability • Allow wound access when necessary • Maintain pin site hygiene • Progress rehabilitation according to stability and healing **Advantages** • Rapid application • Useful in severe soft tissue injury • Useful for open and complex fractures • Allows wound access • Can be adjusted • Useful for lengthening and deformity correction **Disadvantages** • Pin site infection • Bulky frame • Patient discomfort • Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Difficulty with ADLs • Pin loosening • Possible neurovascular injury **Physiotherapy management** • Breathing/circulatory exercises • ROM of free joints • Quadriceps/gluteal isometrics as appropriate • Bed mobility and transfers • Gait training with prescribed assistive device • Progressive strengthening and ROM • Weight bearing only as prescribed • Balance training • Pin site care education **Complications** • Pin site infection • Pin loosening • Osteomyelitis • Joint stiffness • Frame instability • Loss of alignment • Delayed union • Non union • Malunion • Device failure • Nerve/vessel injury • Compartment syndrome **Conclusion** External fixation is particularly valuable when fracture stabilization and soft tissue management must be addressed together Physiotherapy maintains mobility strength and function while protecting the frame --- **5 SPINAL STABILIZATION SURGERIES** **Definition** Spinal stabilization surgery is performed to restore or maintain spinal alignment and stability by fixing one or more vertebral segments often with instrumentation and bone grafting **Indications** • Spinal instability • Progressive scoliosis • Severe kyphosis • Vertebral fractures • Spinal deformity • Spondylolisthesis • Tumours • Severe degenerative instability • Selected spinal infections • Neurological compromise associated with instability • Failed conservative management **Objectives** • Correct deformity • Maintain alignment • Stabilize unstable vertebrae • Prevent progression • Facilitate fusion • Reduce mechanical pain • Protect neurological structures **Harringtons rod system** • Older spinal instrumentation using rods to correct and maintain spinal deformity particularly scoliosis • Principle corrective forces are applied to the spine and maintained while fusion occurs • General procedure posterior exposure instrumentation/hooks rod placement deformity correction bone grafting and closure • Historically important but largely replaced by newer segmental fixation systems **Luques rod system** • Uses contoured rods with sublaminar wires for segmental spinal fixation • Historically used for scoliosis neuromuscular deformity and selected kyphotic/unstable conditions • Provides segmental fixation and control of alignment • Complications include neurological injury infection implant failure loss of correction and pseudarthrosis **Steffee plating** • Older segmental spinal fixation method using plates and screws especially for lumbar stabilization • Pedicle screws are connected by longitudinal plates to stabilize the spine and facilitate fusion • Used in selected lumbar instability spondylolisthesis deformity fractures and pseudarthrosis • Complications include screw malposition neurological/vascular injury infection implant failure and non union **General principles** • Proper patient selection • Identify unstable segments • Correct deformity where necessary • Decompress when indicated • Provide stable fixation • Use bone graft to facilitate fusion • Preserve neurological and vascular structures • Maintain sagittal and coronal alignment • Protect spine postoperatively • Begin safe mobilization and progressive rehabilitation **Physiotherapy management** • Preoperative respiratory neurological postural and strength assessment • Patient education and postoperative mobility training • Early postoperative breathing/circulatory exercises • Log rolling and safe transfers • Appropriate ambulation • Later progressive walking lower limb strengthening balance and functional training • Core stabilization only when permitted • Avoid excessive flexion twisting heavy lifting and high impact activity during healing unless cleared **Complications** • Infection • Neurological deficit • Dural injury • Implant failure • Pseudarthrosis • Loss of correction • Persistent pain • Adjacent segment disease • Spinal stiffness • Thromboembolic complications **Conclusion** Spinal stabilization aims to restore stability correct deformity and achieve solid fusion Harrington and Luque systems are historically important modern fixation generally uses more advanced segmental instrumentation --- **6 LIMB RE ATTACHMENT / LIMB REPLANTATION** **Definition** Limb replantation is a microsurgical procedure in which a completely amputated limb or part is surgically reattached with restoration of bone vascular nerve tendon and soft tissue continuity **Objectives** • Restore limb continuity • Restore blood circulation • Preserve maximum limb length • Restore sensation • Restore motor function • Achieve skeletal union • Obtain useful functional recovery **Indications** • Major limb amputation in selected patients • Thumb amputation • Multiple digit amputation • Amputation through the palm • Paediatric amputation • Multiple digit injury • Selected proximal upper limb amputations • Clean cut amputation with a viable amputated part **Poor candidates / relative contraindications** • Severe crush injury • Severe contamination • Prolonged ischaemia • Extensive tissue destruction • Life threatening associated injuries • Unsuitable amputated part • Severe medical instability **Classification** • Complete vs incomplete amputation • By level finger hand wrist forearm elbow arm toe foot ankle leg knee thigh • By mechanism clean cut crush avulsion and combined crush avulsion **Principles** • Resuscitate and control bleeding • Preserve amputated part correctly • Debride nonviable tissue • Prepare/shorten bone when required • Restore skeletal stability • Repair tendons • Repair arteries and veins • Repair nerves • Provide soft tissue coverage • Monitor circulation closely **Preservation of amputated part** • Wrap in sterile moist gauze • Place in a clean waterproof bag • Place the bag in an ice/cold water mixture • Do not place the amputated part directly on ice or directly in water **Surgical sequence** • Debridement • Bone preparation and fixation • Tendon repair • Arterial microsurgical anastomosis • Venous repair • Nerve repair • Skin/soft tissue coverage • Postoperative vascular monitoring **Physiotherapy management** • Protect repairs and control edema • Maintain safe joint mobility • Respiratory/circulatory exercises • Appropriate isometrics • Progressive ROM after healing • Tendon gliding where appropriate • Strengthening • Sensory re education • Scar and edema management • Fine motor training for upper limb • Gait training for lower limb • Proprioception and functional/return to work training **Complications** • Vascular thrombosis • Arterial occlusion • Venous congestion • Infection • Compartment syndrome • Tissue necrosis • Joint stiffness • Muscle weakness • Tendon adhesions • Non union/malunion • Cold intolerance • Neuroma • Poor sensory/motor recovery • Chronic pain • Functional limitation **Conclusion** Limb replantation is a complex multidisciplinary microsurgical procedure Outcome depends on injury level and mechanism ischaemia time tissue viability quality of repair and prolonged rehabilitation","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in refining tool selection based on those crucial instructions. Specifically, I'm prioritizing direct tool execution and avoiding nested commands, remembering to consider all tools first. I will keep in mind that I should never use `cat` to create a new file or append to an existing one, nor use `grep` nested, or `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, `sed` in ways that are not direct. Explicitly listing related tools before any execution is now paramount.\n\n\n**Evaluating Command Strategies**\n\nI am now assessing different command structures, specifically direct execution versus indirect, nested approaches • 1 goal is to streamline the processes • 2 avoiding unnecessary complexity is key • 3 keeping the commands simple is my focus --- I am working to define rules for optimal command selection to be more efficient in my tool usage • 1 I'm still trying to avoid nested commands and extra steps whenever possible • 2 direct execution is the core focus • 3 I am also trying to keep the commands short and simple\n\n\n**Defining Arthrodesis Criteria**