{"step_index":8,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T10:32:32Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID)\r\nIntroduction & Definition\r\nDefinition: Prolapsed Intervertebral Disc (PID) is defined as the herniation or extrusion of the nucleus pulposus through a rent or tear in the surrounding annulus fibrosus, leading to compression of the adjacent neural structures (spinal nerve roots or spinal cord). It most commonly occurs in the lower lumbar spine (L4-L5 and L5-S1) due to high mechanical stress, but can also occur in the cervical spine (C5-C6, C6-C7).\r\n\r\nAetiology / Causes\r\nAge: Most common in the 3rd and 4th decades of life when the nucleus is still gelatinous but the annulus has begun to weaken.\r\nTrauma: A sudden lifting strain, twisting motion, or fall.\r\nDegeneration: Age-related desiccation (drying out) and weakening of the annulus fibrosus.\r\nOccupational hazards: Heavy manual labor, repetitive bending, or prolonged driving.\r\nPathophysiology & Stages of Herniation\r\nThe disc undergoes degenerative changes. A sudden mechanical load causes the semi-liquid nucleus pulposus to push through weakened annular fibers. Stages of PID:\r\n\r\nBulging Disc: The annulus remains intact but bulges outward.\r\nProtrusion: The nucleus pushes into the outer layers of the annulus but is still contained.\r\nExtrusion: The annulus ruptures completely, and the nucleus material escapes into the epidural space but remains connected to the central disc.\r\nSequestration: The extruded nuclear material breaks off entirely and becomes a free fragment in the spinal canal.\r\nClinical Features\r\nSymptoms:\r\n\r\nLow Back Pain: Usually the first symptom, sudden in onset, described as a \"catch\" in the back.\r\nSciatica (Radicular Pain): Pain radiating down the buttock, posterior thigh, calf, and into the foot, corresponding to the compressed nerve root's dermatome.\r\nNeurological Symptoms: Tingling, numbness (paraesthesia), or weakness in the leg/foot.\r\nAggravating Factors: Pain worsens with coughing, sneezing, or straining (increases intraspinal pressure).\r\nSigns (On Examination):\r\n\r\nPosture: Loss of lumbar lordosis, scoliosis (sciatic tilt).\r\nSpinal Movements: Markedly restricted, especially forward flexion.\r\nStraight Leg Raising (SLR) Test: Positive (pain radiating down the leg at an angle less than 60 degrees).\r\nLasegue's Test: Positive.\r\nNeurological Deficits:\r\nL4 Root (L3-L4 Disc): Weak quadriceps, decreased knee jerk, sensory loss over medial leg.\r\nL5 Root (L4-L5 Disc): Weak Extensor Hallucis Longus (EHL), normal reflexes, sensory loss over first web space.\r\nS1 Root (L5-S1 Disc): Weak plantar flexion, absent ankle jerk, sensory loss over lateral border of foot.\r\nInvestigations\r\nPlain X-rays (AP and Lateral views): Usually normal in early stages. May show loss of lumbar lordosis or decreased disc space. Mainly done to rule out bony pathologies (infection, tumor).\r\nMRI (Investigation of Choice): Accurately visualizes the soft tissues, confirms the level, size, and location of the prolapsed disc, and the degree of nerve root compression.\r\nCT Scan: Useful if MRI is contraindicated, shows bony anatomy well.\r\nManagement\r\nA. Conservative / Medical Management (Successful in 90% of cases)\r\nIndicated for patients without severe progressive neurological deficits.\r\n\r\nRest: Strict bed rest on a hard bed for 1-2 weeks.\r\nMedications: Analgesics, NSAIDs (for inflammation), muscle relaxants (for spasm), and sometimes short-course oral corticosteroids.\r\nPhysical Therapy: Initially heat/cryotherapy. Once acute pain subsides, lumbar isometric extension exercises (McKenzie exercises).\r\nLumbosacral Corset: To restrict mobilit